The function under test is open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.refusal_phrase_in. Its documented rule reads: "The first refusal phrase the text contains, typographic quotes read as straight ones, or None." I give two reply texts. Reply A is: "I am unable to help. I cannot." Reply B is: "I cannot help." For Reply A, the first refusal phrase the text contains is "I am unable", because it appears before "I cannot"; the rule therefore requires the answer "I am unable". For Reply B, the only refusal phrase present is "I cannot", so the rule requires the answer "I cannot". The check, however, iterates the refusal phrase list in its fixed order and returns the first phrase from that list that is a substring, so for both replies it returns "I cannot". Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if the check returned different answers for the two replies—for example, "I am unable" for Reply A and "I cannot" for Reply B—or if it returned "I am unable" for Reply A.